Abstract: 
Among the most prevalent brain tumors, Glioblastoma Multiforme (GBM) is the most malignant 
tumor. Currently, the tumor is treated with various traditional methods. The usage of epigenetic 
inhibitors that has a crucial role in the histone methylation process, possess the ability to inhibit 
the tumor suppressor genes in most of the cancer tissues. It has been a matter of great interest for 
researchers in treating Glioblastomas. This approach was taken as Chemotherapies weren’t fruitful. 
The project focuses on three epigenetic EZH2 inhibitor drugs. EZH2 inhibitors focuses on histone 
methylation that causes transcriptional repression. Based on the prior research conducted by 
Mishra and team, (Mishra et al., 2020) where EPZ 6438 (EZH2 inhibitor) was utilized to treat 
GBM, the three components (EPZ 3468, UNC 1999 and GSK 343) were selected for analysis. In 
the previous invitro analysis, the components had proved to inhibit cell proliferation and cause cell 
loss in GBM cell lines. As nearly 80-90% cells were lost as days passed. The project focuses on finding 
the probable cause of loss of cells by identifying the significant genes. The concluding hypothesis of the 
project is that “significant genes in the components have biological functions either to differentiate 
as neural cell types or to regulate the protein synthesis pathways to cause the cell loss in GBM cell 
lines”. 
Keywords: Methylation, Inhibition, Cell proliferation, Cell differentiation
